> So, what that means is, if you're wearing a +3 chain mail, and you read
> a blessed scroll of enchant armor, there is a 2/3 chance (!) that it
> will be evaporated.  What the hardcore spoiled nethacker does in this
> situation, is to DISENCHANT the armor to +2, and then read the blessed
> scroll for a max of +5.  If you are brave, foolish, and you have lots
> of +5 armor to waste and extra scrolls, you can try for more.  If you're
> trying for +7 from +5, you have only a 1/5 chance of getting it, and a
> 4/5 chance of losing your armor.

Close.  It's perfectly safe to enchant +3 armor; evaporation is only a risk
when the enchantment level is _over_ +3.  A blessed scroll will add one or
two points to the armor, thus allowing you to reach +5.  If you knock it
down to +2, a blessed scroll will add one, two, or three points, so +5 is
still possible, just less likely.  Elven equipment supports two more levels
of enchantment, so you're safe enchanting +5 elven armor.

(Conversely, if your [elven] armor is below [-5] -3, and you read a cursed
scroll of enchant armor, there's a very high probability for it to go poof.
So the lowest you can safely generate is [-6] -4.  Not that I've seen anyone
actually trying for this sort of thing...)

